`lib/WWW/DNSMadeEasy.pm:25-30`: `isa => sub { $_ && ( $_ eq '1.2' or $_ eq '2.0' ) }`. A Moo `isa` gets the value in `$_[0]` (not `$_`) and has to `die` to reject; its return value is ignored. So the check never fires:

```
perl -Ilib -e 'use WWW::DNSMadeEasy; print WWW::DNSMadeEasy->new( api_key => "a", secret => "b", api_version => "9.9" )->api_endpoint'
# https://api.dnsmadeeasy.com/V9.9/
```

A typo such as `api_version => 2` or `'2'` therefore produces 404s from `/V2/` instead of a constructor error.

Done when: the check dies with a message naming the two valid values (and `2`/`2.0` numeric input is either coerced or rejected explicitly); `t/00-load.t` or a new test covers a bad value.

